Application to Real Samples. The developed MOF-199-coated SPME fiber was applied to the analysis of three indoor air samples collected from a furniture market, a newly decorated bedroom, and an office for the benzene homologues. The results
are given in Table 2 and Figure 8. Recoveries obtained by spiking \(720\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) for benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, \(p\)-xylene, styrene, and trimethylbenzene and \(920\,\mathrm{ng}\,\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) for chlorobenzene ranged from \(87\%\) to \(106\%\). Benzene and toluene in sample 1, \(p\)-xylene in samples 2 and 3, and styrene in sample 3 were reliably detected (above the LOQs). Although several other benzene homologues were detected in the three samples, we were not able to reliably measure their individual peak areas
as they were determined to be below the LOQs. Figure 8 shows chromatograms of the benzene homologue standard gas mixture and the three samples with the MOF-199-coated fiber for SPME.
## CONCLUSIONS
We have reported the first example of the utilization of MOFs as SPME fiber coatings for efficient sample pretreatment due to their unique structure and excellent adsorption properties. The developed MOF-199-coated SPME fibers using our in situ growth approach offer large enhancement factors, a wide linear range, and good reproducibility for the SPME of the benzene homologues.
